keccak.h is derived from ethash's lib/keccak/keccak.c,
Copyright 2018 Pawel Bylica, whose sponge in turn builds on the "simple"
Keccak implementation by Ronny Van Keer included in "Reference and
optimized code in C" (https://keccak.team/archives.html, CC0-1.0 /
Public Domain).

The vendored copy is reduced to the sponge alone:

  - The Keccak-f[1600] permutation is not included. It is declared as
    monad_keccakf1600() and left for the consumer to define, so that a
    target with a hardware or proof-system Keccak primitive can supply
    it. The zkVM guest does exactly that in
    zkvm/category/crypto/keccakf1600.h.
  - keccak() is specialised to a 256-bit digest; the bit-width parameter
    and the 512-bit and fixed-size-input entry points are dropped.
  - The sponge is exposed as monad_keccak256(), matching the signature
    of category/crypto/keccak.h, so that it drops in as an alternative
    definition of monad_crypto's keccak256. It therefore writes into a
    caller-supplied byte buffer rather than returning a union
    ethash_hash256, and the vendored header declares no types.
  - The sponge lives in the header as an always_inline static, rather
    than in a translation unit, so that it constant-folds at the call
    sites that hash a fixed-length key.
  - The load_le() and to_le64() helpers become prefixed static inline
    functions, so that the header exports no macros; upstream's fallback
    macro for compilers lacking __builtin_memcpy is dropped with them.
  - The ALWAYS_INLINE macro from ethash's lib/support/attributes.h is
    dropped rather than vendoring that header for a single use.

